@_jamico - agree.  The tricky thing is figuring out what exactly is a “central party” in this context.  “… a group of persons or entities having operational, economic, or voting control of a crypto system” (the definition in the release) has limited utility here because there will almost always be some “group of persons” who, acting together, have functional control over the system, even if that group is highly decentralized. And with many projects, those persons with functional control are not particularly decentralized. This unfortunately leads us back to the Hinman question: so when is the system “sufficiently decentralized”?

Title I of the Clarity Act would have neatly sidestepped this problem by focusing the securities law analysis on tokens that have a legal relationship with a company (or companies), including a promise to use corporate revenue to buy back a token - a concept we all agree has no place in the crypto ecosystem.

For now though, good judgment and  common sense will have to prevail.

